Bathygauze

Ingredients: INDUSTRIAL ROCK / METAL + ETHEREAL, DREAMY & AIRY + Tenor Saxophone
Creative Catalyst: The sound was discovered by deep-sea divers repairing transatlantic data cables who tapped into acoustic crosstalk vibrating through armored benthic conduits.

Bathygauze is the sound of crushing hydraulic pressure meeting weightless pelagic suspension: monolithic, down-tuned industrial metal grooves and distorted sheet-metal percussion submerged beneath drifting oceans of shoegaze reverb, sliced open by the reedy, overdriven cry of a tenor saxophone that behaves more like a fractured human voice than a brass instrument.

Genre DNA

- Tempo & Pulse: 68–84 BPM. Half-time, sludgy industrial stomps driven by seismic 808 sub-impacts and dragging snare clanks, counterbalanced by fluid, unmetered ambient drift.
- Harmonic Palette: Aeolian and Phrygian modes drenched in suspended 2nds and major 7ths; brutal low-register drone roots clashing against crystalline, open-string guitar tapestries.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Sub-pressure gating: Industrial rhythm guitars are sidechained to ultra-low hydrophone sub-rumble, creating a suffocating "pumping" effect like water displacement.
  2. Resonant-bell saturation: Tenor saxophones are close-miked with contact transducers inside the bell, driven through rat-distortion pedals and washed out in 15-second shimmer reverbs.
  3. Spectral cable hum: Authentic transatlantic telecommunication frequencies and high-voltage cable hiss serve as continuous high-frequency atmospheric beds.
- Dynamics: Abrupt oceanic abysses; verses hover in suspended, airless vocal murmurs and breathy sax flutters before exploding without warning into tectonic walls of industrial fuzz and piston-driven percussion.

Scene & Origin Lore

Bathygauze solidified along the wind-scoured coast of the Outer Hebrides in the late 1990s, when deep-sea technician and ambient tape-loop experimenter Eilidh MacCallum began collaborating with out-of-work shipbuilders and punk musicians in Stornoway. Having spent months aboard cable-laying vessels listening to rhythmic hydro-acoustic leakage through submerged fiber-optic armatures, MacCallum brought acoustic field recordings into gutted herring-curing sheds. The scene quickly developed a stark, utilitarian culture: audiences dressed in salt-stained rubber deck boots, thermal base layers, and sheer oilskin smocks, gathering in unheated boat houses lit solely by amber sodium-vapor work lamps. Concerts were ritualistic and sensory; audiences stood dead silent during ethereal, airborne saxophone preludes, bracing themselves for the speaker-shattering impact of scrap-metal percussion that shook loose sea salt from the roof beams.

Typical Instruments: Selmer Mark VI Tenor Saxophone (run through dual pitch-shifters, tape delay, and fuzz), 7-string baritone guitars tuned to drop-A, Ensoniq ASR-10 sampler (loaded with pneumatic punches and underwater cable friction), Moog Minitaur, heavily processed hammered scrap metal, and ethereal, whisper-soft soprano vocals.

For Fans Of: Nine Inch Nails, Cocteau Twins, Colin Stetson, Godflesh

